Of course on a multiuser system, that is _still_ true.

Any competition about the score table positions is
user-induced, and exterior to the play of the game.

The NetHack game is still a one player game, and the
amount of tedium that one player is willing to
endure has no effect on adding tedium to other
player's games.

[Notice also that by now, with the game easily
downloaded and installed on a wide variety of single
user hardware, playing NetHack on multiuser systems
is probably a tiny minority of all NetHack play, and
so not a good basis for arguments about how the game
"should" work. Even in households sharing a machine,
like Raise's, there probably isn't horribly
cut-throat competition between parents and children
for score table slots.]

